{"items":[{"targetId":"split-000051","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Retaining each agent's role means the rewrite preserves how every external connection can still interact with the surviving local structure.","densityScore":0.71,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains contextual preservation as preservation of external connectivity."}]},{"targetId":"split-000052","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"Equal levels identify the two replicators as operating in the same sharing context, so their duplication structures can cancel instead of being propagated through one another.","densityScore":0.78,"relation":"supports","reason":"Connects the level-equality condition to annihilation."}]},{"targetId":"split-000053","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Reconnecting corresponding auxiliary ports preserves the paths that had passed through the two removed replicators.","densityScore":0.73,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains why auxiliary-port reconnection is part of annihilation."}]},{"targetId":"split-000054","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"Annihilation is a cancellation rewrite: it removes redundant sharing structure rather than distributing either net further.","densityScore":0.74,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Contrasts annihilation with replication-producing commutation."}]},{"targetId":"split-000055","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"Matching levels alone does not determine a valid hand-built annihilation redex, because port count and level shifts must also align at every corresponding connection.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"supports","reason":"Clarifies why the additional structural constraints matter."}]},{"targetId":"split-000056","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Commutation moves differently scoped sharing structures past one another while preserving the distinction between their levels.","densityScore":0.74,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains the operational meaning of the alternative rewrite."}]},{"targetId":"split-000057","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"One new higher-level replica is needed for each branch because each auxiliary port represents a distinct continuation of the lower-level replicator's sharing structure.","densityScore":0.76,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains the per-auxiliary-port replication count."}]},{"targetId":"split-000058","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"The lower-level replicas preserve the higher-level replicator's local shape at each branch, while the paired higher-level replicas account for the level shift of that branch.","densityScore":0.77,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Relates exact lower-level copies to the asymmetric commutation rewrite."}]},{"targetId":"split-000059","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Adding the exit-port delta transports a replica into the level context represented by the branch it follows.","densityScore":0.78,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Gives the semantic role of delta addition."}]},{"targetId":"split-000060","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"An exact copy preserves both the copied structure and its level annotations.","densityScore":0.72,"relation":"fills","reason":"Makes exactness concrete for later duplicate-versus-replicate terminology."}]},{"targetId":"split-000061","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"Replication is broader than duplication because it may preserve structure while transforming levels according to the traversed port deltas.","densityScore":0.77,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Distinguishes the two terms through the role of levels."}]},{"targetId":"split-000062","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"A port delta is an integer level adjustment applied when structure is propagated through that port.","densityScore":0.74,"relation":"enables","reason":"Defines how negative, zero, and positive values affect replicas."}]},{"targetId":"split-000063","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"A bijection gives both a syntax-to-net translation and a unique inverse reading of canonical nets back into terms.","densityScore":0.75,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Unpacks the two directions of the claimed correspondence."}]},{"targetId":"split-000066","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Inductive translation makes each term constructor responsible for wiring together already translated child fragments.","densityScore":0.76,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains how subterm translations compose."}]},{"targetId":"split-000067","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"The incoming wire is the fragment's result connection, while outgoing wires expose the fragment's free-variable interface.","densityScore":0.77,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Assigns roles to the two interface directions."}]},{"targetId":"split-000070","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"A parent constructor consumes the child's incoming result wire and combines the child's exposed variable wires with its own interface.","densityScore":0.76,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains compositional plugging through the fragment interface."}]},{"targetId":"split-000072","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"A bound occurrence is represented by a wire to its binder's parameter structure, so no separately named interface agent is needed.","densityScore":0.76,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains the absence of a variable agent for bound uses."}]},{"targetId":"split-000074","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"The replicator turns one binder connection into several branch connections, one for each occurrence of the bound variable.","densityScore":0.78,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains why multiple uses require a replicator."}]},{"targetId":"split-000077","injections":[{"type":"warrant","text":"With one auxiliary port and no level change, the replicator performs neither branching nor level transformation, so a direct wire has the same effect.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"supports","reason":"Justifies omitting the degenerate replicator."}]},{"targetId":"split-000079","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"The eraser records that the abstraction receives an argument whose bound parameter has no occurrence in the body.","densityScore":0.78,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Connects the unused parameter to erasure behavior."}]},{"targetId":"split-000083","injections":[{"type":"example","text":"This separates the body's computation path from the two distinct demand paths created by the two occurrences of x.","densityScore":0.78,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains the purpose of the three connections in the example."}]},{"targetId":"split-000087","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"Connecting the function fragment at the principal port places a translated abstraction and application in the active configuration required for β-reduction.","densityScore":0.8,"relation":"supports","reason":"Explains why this particular application wiring exposes a redex."}]},{"targetId":"split-000091","injections":[{"type":"interpretation","text":"Increasing the argument level distinguishes the argument's sharing context from the function path without changing the term's syntax.","densityScore":0.76,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Explains the purpose of the argument-level increment."}]},{"targetId":"split-000092","injections":[{"type":"context","text":"The levels are static annotations of the translated net that guide replication and commutation rewrites.","densityScore":0.77,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Separates level annotations from runtime environment data."}]},{"targetId":"split-000097","injections":[{"type":"mechanism","text":"The negative delta returns one replicated branch to a lower sharing level, while the positive delta moves the other branch to a higher one.","densityScore":0.76,"relation":"elaborates","reason":"Interprets the two signed deltas operationally."}]}]}
